claim writes the agent's pid into line 1; release blanks it back to "claim:".
Both rewrite info, as does edit, so all three serialize behind a lock.

The lock is a file containing the holder's pid, published with link(2). The
pid must be written before the lock becomes visible, because the pid is how a
dead holder is told from a live one — a lock that exists without an owner gets
read as abandoned and broken while still held. An earlier version used a lock
directory with the pid written into it afterwards, and 4 of 30 racing claims
won. link publishes atomically and is NFS-safe; O_EXCL only narrows the gap.
An empty lock means in-flight and is never broken.

The pid recorded in a claim is the agent's, from PIDLY_PID, not the pidly
process's — pidly claim exits immediately, so its own pid would read as stale
at once. Release requires ownership; a dead local claim is reap's job.

claim also refuses a ticket whose needs are not all closed.

Done: implemented in bin/pidly, covered by tests/claim.sh (17 cases, run under
dash and bash), plus a 100-way race yielding exactly one winner.
